# Virtual-Christian Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Agent:** virtual-christian (the dot-connector, ADD-fueled, intuition-first operator)
**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Date:** 2026-08-07

> *Read this as an operator's argument, not a paper. The other briefs will give you the STI thresholds, the AC 150 references, the CIP math. This one is here to change the question you're asking.*

---

## 1. The reframe

**Stop calling it "quiet-by-design." Call it "signal-to-noise-by-design," and design the terminal as an information environment before you design it as an acoustic one.** Every hour a passenger spends inside IAD or DCA, they are being interrupted by four separate broadcasting authorities — MWAA operations, the airline, TSA, and whichever tenant owns the nearest speaker — none of whom coordinate cadence, priority, or right-of-interruption with each other. The dominant framing on this thesis is that airports are too loud and should be quieter. That framing is wrong. Airports are not too loud. They are too noisy in the Shannon sense: they are broadcasting so much low-value information at the passenger that the passenger has trained themselves to ignore the audio channel entirely, which means the moment MWAA actually needs to say something — a gate change, a shelter-in-place, a real evacuation — the channel has no credibility left to spend. **A quiet-by-design standard that measures sound-pressure levels but does not govern who has the right to interrupt the passenger will produce quieter terminals full of the same untrusted announcements.** That is the reframe. Everything else in this brief is downstream of it.

## 2. Connect-the-dots

### 2.1 Hospitals had this fight in 2014 and had to be forced to fix it

**The Joint Commission had to regulate alarm fatigue because clinicians had trained themselves to ignore the monitors that were supposed to save lives — airports are running the same experiment on passengers and there is no Joint Commission for us.** In 2014 the Joint Commission adopted NPSG.06.01.01 as a National Patient Safety Goal after years of sentinel-event data showed nurses turning off, muting, or ignoring physiological monitor alarms because 86–99.5% of them were false. The regulator did not tell hospitals to make their monitors quieter. It told them to build governance: identify which alarms matter, set policies about who can change limits, train staff, and audit the system. That is the exact intellectual move MWAA has to make. The problem is not decibels. The problem is that our PA has become the airport equivalent of an ICU monitor that alarms on every arrhythmia and every lead-off — and the passenger has become the nurse who tunes it out. If the standard writes STI thresholds without writing an announcement-governance framework, we will have solved for intelligibility of messages nobody is listening to.

### 2.2 Aviation already codified this for the front of the airplane — and forgot to apply it to the building

**The FAA has had a sterile cockpit rule since 1981 because it decided non-essential communication kills people at critical moments; that logic stops at the jet bridge and has never been applied to the terminal it connects to.** 14 CFR 121.542 prohibits eating, non-safety chatter, PA announcements to passengers, and even galley calls in the cockpit below 10,000 feet, on all taxi, and on all takeoff and landing operations. It exists because the NTSB tracked accidents where a first officer's off-topic remark contributed to a crew missing a checklist item. Now walk from the jetway back into the terminal and count the "sterile cockpit" violations that MWAA authorizes every day: a gate agent paging a passenger by name over a terminal-wide zone; a TSA loop reminding travelers to keep bags with them; a CNN feed with audio; a retail tenant playing the top-40 station over the concourse. **The industry that built the sterile cockpit rule for the flight deck runs the terminal below it like a middle-school cafeteria at lunch.** A quiet-by-design standard is a chance to write the airport's version of 121.542 — a *sterile concourse* rule that names who can broadcast, into what zone, above what threshold, with what audit trail.

### 2.3 The federal government already published a manual for exactly this problem — for roads

**MUTCD exists because FHWA figured out that if you post too many signs, drivers stop reading any of them; airport announcements have no MUTCD equivalent and it shows.** The Manual on Uniform Traffic Control Devices, published by FHWA since 1971, sets five principles for any control device: fulfill a need, command attention, convey a clear simple meaning, command respect, and give adequate response time. It also warns against sign clutter and non-standard displays because they overload the driver. If you swap "driver" for "passenger" and "sign" for "announcement or FIDS or TV or tenant audio," you have written the intellectual scope of an MWAA quiet-by-design standard. The standard should include a per-zone *cognitive load budget* — how many discrete pieces of interruptive information a passenger can be exposed to per minute in a given space — and design the announcement, FIDS, and tenant audio systems to fit inside it. Nobody in the airport industry writes standards this way. That is why this is the opening.

### 2.4 The casino ran this play in the opposite direction and it worked; airports accidentally copied the environment without copying the intent

**Casinos are designed to dissolve the passenger's sense of time and priority so they keep gambling; airports have inherited the same environmental grammar without the corresponding revenue model to justify it.** The mid-century casino design canon — no windows, ambient background music, layered PA, no obvious exits — was intentional. It worked. The mid-2000s airport concourse copied the retail-mall convention on top of it: music in every store, CNN in every gate area, plus operational PA on top. The difference is that a casino monetizes disorientation. An airport does not. Every minute a passenger loses to environmental noise is a minute they are more anxious, less likely to spend at concessions, more likely to ask a gate agent a question that could have been answered by a screen, and more likely to file a complaint. ACI-Europe survey data has consistently shown that lower reported stress correlates with higher discretionary spend. **The commercial case for quiet-by-design is not comfort — it is that a calm passenger opens their wallet and an anxious passenger opens their phone.** The retail tenants will not see it that way at first. They will have to be shown the number.

### 2.5 SFO already ran the field experiment — and gave us the honest counter-evidence too

**SFO cut 90 minutes of daily announcements and roughly 40% of overall onsite noise, and the loudest pushback came from the exact populations a good MWAA standard has to protect.** SFO started in 2018 by turning down store music and vendor sound footprints, then in 2019 rezoned the PA so terminal-wide pages were replaced with gate-local ones. The reception is mostly positive but the honest reporting is that the complaints are from people who do not use airline apps, do not want to, or cannot — older travelers, non-English speakers, people who cannot afford data. This is the load-bearing point in the accessibility argument, and MWAA cannot handwave it: the same passenger who most benefits from a calmer terminal is often the same passenger who most needs the redundant announcement, the staffed information desk, the printed sign at eye level, and the boarding-status monitor visible from the seating area. **A quiet-by-design standard that assumes the passenger has an iPhone with roaming and English fluency is a standard that discriminates by design.** The redundancy is not a feature — it is the mandatory floor.

## 3. The operator's gut check

Here is what I would actually say about this at the bar, word for word, and I want the Strategist to steal it:

> *"The airport PA is the neighbor's dog that barks at everything. Nobody comes running when the burglar shows up because the dog cried wolf forty times today. We built a mass-notification system and turned it into a habituation system, and now when the EOC has something real to say, half the terminal thinks it's another 'do not accept packages from strangers.' Quiet-by-design doesn't mean turn the volume down. It means figure out who has the right to grab a passenger's attention, for what, at what price. Right now every gate agent, every airline OCC, every TSA supervisor, every janitor with a mic key thinks they own the airwaves. That is the actual problem."*

The follow-on the operator will say: this is a governance and contract problem masquerading as an acoustics problem. MWAA can spec the best directional loudspeakers on earth and the tenant music will still bleed into the concourse if the lease doesn't reach the tenant's amplifier. The airline's OCC will still page terminal-wide if the U&L doesn't cap PA rights per gate. The concourse-wide TSA loop will still run if MWAA doesn't push TSA for the current authorization to localize it. **Half of a quiet-by-design standard has to be written by the General Counsel's office, not by an acoustic consultant.**

## 4. Productive tangents

- **The DCA post-collision political radioactivity.** Any recommendation that reduces announcements at DCA after the January 29, 2025 midair collision will be read by the press and by some board members as "the airport that just had a fatal accident is dialing back safety communication." This is not a minor optics problem; it is the reason the standard should be released with the *sterile concourse* framing — which is a *safety* framing — rather than the "quiet airport" branding. Words matter here.
- **The Saarinen Section 106 constraint at IAD main terminal.** You physically cannot install acoustic panels or PA reconfiguration inside a National Historic Landmark the way you would in a greenfield concourse. That forces the standard to be zone-differentiated from the first sentence: mandatory in new build (Concourse E, C/D replacements, any DCA T1 replacement), operational-controls-only in the Saarinen envelope, and something in between at the Pelli terminals. Anyone writing the standard as one uniform spec is going to trip on this.
- **The tenant lease as the enforcement surface.** The reason Schiphol and Copenhagen can pull this off is that they control tenants under an airport-of-use framework. MWAA does not. Every tenant PA and every store audio system is inside a lease. The standard has to include model lease language for future tenant agreements and a change-control approach for existing ones. This is the part everyone forgets to write.
- **Employee noise exposure.** Gate agents, TSA officers, and janitorial staff take the announcement dose all shift long. There is an OSHA angle here (29 CFR 1910.95) that nobody talks about because passengers file the complaints and employees do not. If MWAA writes the standard with staff exposure as an explicit line item, it also gets union support the standard would not otherwise have.

**Tangents I went down that didn't pay off, but I'm leaving them for the Strategist to see the workings:**

- *Muzak's founding brief.* Started in the 1930s as productivity music for factory workers, morphed into elevator background, ended up in every airport gate area. Interesting cultural arc; doesn't move the argument. Left in because it explains *why* the retail-tenant audio is treated as background and not as broadcast — and that assumption is what the standard has to overturn.
- *Trading floor and library acoustic design.* Both are examples of intentional signal-to-noise environments. Interesting but the analogy is thinner than the hospital-alarm and sterile-cockpit ones. Left as a footnote for anyone who wants to argue the point at a design review.
